---
/**
 * /training/agentic-101: the 101's own landing page (website brief, 29 Oct
 * 2026), written to the course-page playbook: what and who, the facts and the
 * one ask on the first screen, "is this for you", the problem, what you'll do,
 * how it runs, a paragraph to forward, then the terms and the ask again.
 * Booking is its only ask: the header, hero, closing band and footer all carry
 * the same 101 ask (asks.ts). Until the booking link exists that ask is an
 * email about a seat; set BOOKING_101_URL in site.ts and every placement
 * switches.
 *
 * Open facts (the exact day, online or in person) come from PILOT_101 in
 * training.ts and read as "I'll confirm" until they're set, never as a guess.
 * The price comes only through the private price gate (priceFor, keyed
 * "agentic-101"); without it every price line says how to get the price.
 *
 * Demos: the brief wants them here before the 1 Dec invitations. None exists
 * yet, so the section isn't rendered; add it above the paragraph to forward
 * when the first demo is ready.
 */
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Chapter from "../../components/Chapter.astro";
import Offer from "../../components/Offer.astro";
import { askLink, has101Booking, invoice101Href } from "../../config/asks";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from "../../config/site";
import { PILOT_101 as pilot, course, priceFor } from "../../config/training";

const intro = course("agentic-101");
const price = priceFor("agentic-101");
const heroAsk = askLink("book-101", "101-hero");
const bandAsk = askLink("book-101", "101-band");
const linkAttrs = (a: typeof heroAsk, placement: string) => ({
  href: a.href,
  target: a.external ? "_blank" : undefined,
  rel: a.external ? "noopener noreferrer" : undefined,
  "data-track": a.event,
  "data-placement": placement,
});

// When and where, written out in "How it runs". An open fact says when it
// will be confirmed.
const stillOpen = [!pilot.day && "the day", !pilot.format && "whether it's online or in person"].filter(Boolean);
const whenWhere = `It runs on ${pilot.day ?? `a morning in ${pilot.week}`}, ${pilot.time}${pilot.format ? `, ${pilot.format}` : ""}.${stillOpen.length ? ` I'll confirm ${stillOpen.join(", and ")} when booking opens.` : ""}`;
const cost = price
  ? `${price} + VAT a seat, the founding rate for this first run.`
  : has101Booking
    ? "A founding rate for this first run. The price, with VAT, is on the booking page."
    : "A founding rate for this first run. Ask for a seat and I'll send you the price.";
const forwardPrice = price
  ? `A seat is ${price} + VAT, a founding rate for the first run.`
  : has101Booking
    ? "Seats are at a founding rate for the first run, and the price is on the booking page."
    : `Seats are at a founding rate for the first run; for the price, email ${CONTACT_EMAIL}.`;
const forwardWhen = pilot.day ? `on ${pilot.day}` : `on a morning in ${pilot.week}`;
// The first screen's fact line: the instrument voice, compact enough that the
// facts and the ask sit above the fold on a laptop.
const heroFacts = [
  `Half a day, ${pilot.time}`,
  pilot.day ?? pilot.week.replace(/^the /, ""),
  pilot.format ?? "online or in person, to be confirmed",
  `runs with ${pilot.minSeats} to ${pilot.maxSeats} people`,
  price ? `${price} + VAT a seat, founding rate` : "founding rate",
].join(" · ");
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`${intro.title}: a half-day course - burningsuit`}
  description="A half-day course for people who build in Power BI but don't write code: hand a change to an AI agent, check its work, and keep the decisions about what your numbers mean."
  contactSubject={`The ${intro.title}`}
  ask="book-101"
>
  <Fragment slot="footer-invitation">{has101Booking ? "Book by card, or by invoice if your organisation needs one. Either way, it's me you'll hear from" : "Tell me what you'd want to hand to an agent first, and I'll tell you whether the 101 will get you there. Replies come from me"}</Fragment>

  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1 class="enter appear-300">{intro.title}</h1>
      <p class="lead enter appear-450">A half-day course for people who build in Power BI but don't write code. You'll hand a change to an AI agent (a tool that makes the change itself and shows you what it did), check its work, and keep the decisions about what your numbers mean, such as what counts as a sale.</p>
      <p class="offer-meta enter appear-450">{heroFacts}</p>
      <div class="cta-row routes">
        <a class="cta" {...linkAttrs(heroAsk, "101-hero")}>{heroAsk.label}</a>
      </div>
      {has101Booking ? (
        <p class="hero-sig">{price ? "You'll pay by card on a secure Stripe page." : "You'll see the price, with VAT, on a secure Stripe page before you pay."} If your organisation pays by invoice or purchase order, <a href={invoice101Href}>email me</a> and I'll invoice it.</p>
      ) : (
        <p class="hero-sig">The email comes straight to me. I'll reply with the price, and send you the day and the booking link as soon as they're set.</p>
      )}
      <ul class="routes enter appear-450">
        <li><a class="route" href="#who">Is this for you?</a></li>
        <li><a class="route" href="#what">What you'll do</a></li>
        <li><a class="route" href="#details">How it runs</a></li>
        <li><a class="route" href="#for-whoever-pays">To send to your manager</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="thread" data-reveal-raw aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <Chapter id="who" kicker="Is this for you?">
    <p>You'll need to have built a report in Power BI, even a simple one. You won't need to write code, and you won't need to have used an agent before. You'll be the one asking for each change and checking what the agent did.</p>
    <p>If you're a Power BI developer who doesn't write code, it's for you too. If you already work with coding agents every day, it will cover ground you know.</p>
  </Chapter>

  {/* ALAN: the problem paragraph is drafted from the brief, readers' own
      words in meeting notes, and your 27 Oct "wild west" line; make the
      first person yours, ideally with one thing an agent now does well that
      it didn't before. */}
  <Chapter id="problem" kicker="Agents aren't only for developers">
    <p>You build the reports because someone had to. You've probably asked ChatGPT or Copilot in Power BI for a DAX measure and got one that looked right and didn't work in your report. And everything you read about AI agents building Power BI seems written for people who code. So it's easy to decide agents aren't for you, or to worry that one will break something in the background that you won't be able to find.</p>
    <p>Not long ago, that was fair. Having an agent build something in Power BI was the wild west: full of bugs, and not quite what you'd asked for. I don't think that's true any more, as long as you know which decisions you're handing over and how to check what comes back. That's what this half-day teaches.</p>
  </Chapter>

  {/* ALAN: three items from the brief's three capabilities; confirm them
      against the session plan, and add or swap items (up to six). */}
  <Chapter id="what" kicker="What you'll do">
    <p>You'll work on a Power BI report I've prepared for teaching.</p>
    <div class="offer-table">
      <Offer title="Hand over a change">
        <p>You'll ask an agent, in plain English, to make a change to the report, such as adding a measure, and watch it work. Back at your desk, you'll have seen a change go from request to result, so you can judge what to try on your own reports.</p>
      </Offer>
      <Offer title="Check what it did">
        <p>You'll read what the agent changed and compare the numbers before and after, so you can catch a change that runs without an error and still answers the wrong question. Back at your desk, you can do the same before you trust a change, whoever made it.</p>
      </Offer>
      <Offer title="Sort what to hand over from what to keep">
        <p>Some decisions are about building, such as how a measure is written. Others are about what your numbers mean, such as what counts as a sale. You'll practise telling them apart and keeping the second kind for yourself. Back at your desk, you'll find it easier to decide what to hand to an agent and what to keep.</p>
      </Offer>
    </div>
  </Chapter>

  <Chapter id="details" kicker="How it runs">
    <div class="offer-table">
      <Offer id="when" title="When and where" meta={pilot.day ? `${pilot.day} · ${pilot.time}` : `${pilot.week.charAt(0).toUpperCase()}${pilot.week.slice(1)} · ${pilot.time}`}>
        <p>{whenWhere}</p>
      </Offer>
      <Offer id="how-long" title="How long, and how many" meta={`${intro.length} · ${pilot.minSeats} to ${pilot.maxSeats} people`}>
        <p>It's a public course, so you book a seat for yourself and can come on your own. It runs with {pilot.minSeats} to {pilot.maxSeats} people.</p>
      </Offer>
      <Offer id="how-much" title="How much" meta={price ? `${price} + VAT a seat` : "Founding rate"}>
        <p>{cost} If your organisation pays by invoice or purchase order, tell me and I'll send an invoice.</p>
      </Offer>
    </div>
    <p>I teach it myself. It runs on a Power BI report and data I've built for teaching, so you won't bring your own reports or data, and none of your organisation's data is needed.</p>
    {/* ALAN: what delegates need installed, which agent, and any licence. */}
    <p>Before the day, I'll send you what to install and a way to check it works.</p>
    <p>Afterwards, I'll ask what worked for you and what didn't. This is the first run, and your answers shape the next one.</p>
  </Chapter>

  <Chapter id="for-whoever-pays" kicker="To send to your manager">
    {/* The paragraph to forward: written to stand on its own when pasted into
        an email to a manager or L&D, in the third person about who'd go. */}
    {/* <wbr> lets the address wrap on a narrow phone without adding
        characters to a copied paragraph.
        copy-lint-ignore agentic: part of the page's address, not prose */}
    <p>{intro.title} is a half-day course from burningsuit for people who build reports in Power BI but don't write code. They'll hand a Power BI change to an AI agent (a tool that makes the change itself and shows what it did), check what it did, and learn which decisions to keep for themselves, working on prepared examples, not live company data. It runs {forwardWhen}, {pilot.time}, {pilot.format ?? "online or in person (to be confirmed)"}, for {pilot.minSeats} to {pilot.maxSeats} people. {forwardPrice} Details{has101Booking ? " and booking" : ""}: https://<wbr />burningsuit.co.uk/<wbr />training/<wbr />agentic-101/</p>
  </Chapter>

  {/* Terms: the pilot's conditions (29 Oct 2026). Refund and transfer terms
      for someone who can't come are open; add them here before
      BOOKING_101_URL is set. */}
  <Chapter id="terms" kicker="Will it run?">
    <p>This is the first run, at a founding rate. It goes ahead with at least {pilot.minSeats} paid seats, and I'll decide on {pilot.goNoGo}. If there are fewer by then, it moves once, to a date in {pilot.movesTo}, and everyone who has booked chooses between that date and a refund.</p>
  </Chapter>

  <section class="cta-band" aria-label={bandAsk.label}>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="cta-invite">
        {has101Booking ? (
          <p class="cta-note">Booking takes you to a secure card payment. Paying by invoice or purchase order? <a href={invoice101Href}>Email me</a> instead.</p>
        ) : (
          <p class="cta-note">Nothing is booked or paid until you decide. I'll reply with the price, and send the day and the booking link as soon as they're set.</p>
        )}
        <a class="cta" {...linkAttrs(bandAsk, "101-band")}>{bandAsk.label}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="thread thread-end" data-reveal-raw aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="talk-spacer"></div>
</BaseLayout>
